hls: a seek past a discontinuity finds its place

After a seek the reader opens the segment the time falls in, which it
finds by adding up the lengths of the segments before it, and then lets
go of every packet whose timestamp is below the time asked for. Where a
stream's timestamps start again at an EXT-X-DISCONTINUITY, as they do in
a film stitched together from parts, the packets of every later part
have timestamps that say nothing of their place in the playlist. They
were all below the time asked for, all let go of, and the seek ended at
the end of the file.

The reader now counts the discontinuities before each segment. A seek in
a complete playlist that lands past one takes the first packet it reads
there as the start of that segment, which it is, and from then on moves
every timestamp by the difference: for the comparison, and for the
caller, who asked for a time and now gets packets at that time. One
shift serves all the playlists of a presentation, which restart
together. A seek to before the first discontinuity is as it was.

